      "text": "$a^{2^n} = a^n \\cdot a^n = \\left(a \\cdot a^{n-1}\\right)^2, a,a^{n-1},n \\in \\mathbb{R}\\,.$ Hence, since \\(x + y = 2\\), the given equation is \n\n\\[\n16^{x + y} + 16^{x + y} = 1 \\Longrightarrow 2\\cdot 16^x = 1 \\Longrightarrow 16^x = \\frac{1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x + y = 2\\), simplifying this yields\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot 6^{y}=1 \\Longrightarrow 6^y = \\frac{1}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich in turn implies \\(6^y = 2^{-1}\\). Since \\(-1\\cdot 3^y = 2 \\cdot 3^{-y}\\), raising both sides to the power \\(y\\) and removing all powers of the same numbers to extend the determinant yields \\(yx - 3^y + 3^{-y} - 3^x + 3^y = \\frac{1}{2}.\\)\n\nBy contradiction, the function \\(f(t) = -t^3 + t - 1\\) has a single root. However, since we are looking at odd symmetry about \\(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5-\\sqrt{53}}{12}}\\), only one root of \\(f\\) lies on this segment. The function \\(f(t) = 2t\\) therefore has one and only one point where \\(f(t) = g(t)\\), that is, \\(h(x,y) = f(x) - g(x,y) = 4 \\left(t - c_1\\right)^2 + y^2 - x^2\\), yielding \\(h\\left( x, y\\right) = 4\\left(x^2 + y^2 - y^2\\right) = 4(x^2 + y^2 - 2(x \\cdot y)) = -4(y -  \\frac{x^2}{y})\\). Therefore scaling gives \\(x^2 + y^2 - 2(x \\cdot y) = -\\frac{5\\sqrt{53}-19}{24} \\Longrightarrow x^2 + y^2 + 3 = -\\frac{43 \\sqrt{53} +57}{24 \\cdot \\sqrt{3}}\\). It follows that we have:\n\n\\[\n56\\left[h(x, y)\\right]^2 + 16 \\left[\\left(x-x\\cdot\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5-\\sqrt{53}}{12}}\\right)^2 + \\left(y - y \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5-\\sqrt{53}}{12}}\\right)^2\\right] = 16^{\\frac{7}{18}}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}.\n\\]",
